WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,882
Hardening starts with knowing what exists.

2
00:00:02,882 --> 00:00:19,003
A useful inventory includes routers, switches, firewalls, wireless controllers, VPN gateways, servers, appliances, cloud networks, subnets, VLANs, DNS and DHCP services, NTP sources, third-party connections, and management interfaces.

3
00:00:19,003 --> 00:00:26,479
It should also identify what each asset does, where it lives, what it depends on, and whether it is still supported.

4
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:31,074
Unknown assets are hard to patch, hard to monitor, and hard to protect.

5
00:00:31,074 --> 00:00:33,722
Ownership matters as much as discovery.

6
00:00:33,722 --> 00:00:40,264
Every network segment, rule set, service, and connection should have a responsible team or person.

7
00:00:40,264 --> 00:00:49,766
If a firewall rule allows traffic between a user subnet and a server network, someone should understand why it exists and who can approve changes.

8
00:00:49,766 --> 00:00:55,373
If a VPN profile grants administrative access, someone should own the access review.

9
00:00:55,373 --> 00:01:01,448
Without ownership, hardening questions become slow and risky during production pressure.

10
00:01:01,448 --> 00:01:08,145
Network maps do not need to be perfect on day one, but they must be accurate enough to support decisions.

11
00:01:08,145 --> 00:01:21,229
A practical map shows critical paths: user networks, server networks, management networks, guest wireless, cloud networks, internet edges, firewalls, remote access paths, and key dependencies.

12
00:01:21,229 --> 00:01:31,354
It should help answer operational questions such as what depends on this subnet, what would break if this rule changed, and where monitoring should exist.

13
00:01:31,354 --> 00:01:33,924
Inventory is not a static spreadsheet.

14
00:01:33,924 --> 00:01:43,036
It should be reviewed when projects launch, systems retire, sites change, cloud networks are added, or emergency exceptions are created.

15
00:01:43,036 --> 00:01:47,397
A network that was accurate last quarter may already have drifted.

16
00:01:47,397 --> 00:01:59,079
Regular review turns hardening from guesswork into managed operations and gives teams a safer starting point for exposure reduction, segmentation, patching, and validation.
